1
00:00:00,060 --> 00:00:03,780
This is chymotrypsin, a digestive
enzyme made in the pancreas.

2
00:00:03,820 --> 00:00:05,940
Its job is to cut peptide bonds,

3
00:00:05,980 --> 00:00:07,860
and it is selective: it cuts on the

4
00:00:07,900 --> 00:00:10,900
carboxyl side of large
hydrophobic residues,

5
00:00:10,940 --> 00:00:13,700
phenylalanine, tyrosine and tryptophan.

6
00:00:13,740 --> 00:00:17,300
The bond an enzyme cuts
is called a scissile bond,

7
00:00:17,340 --> 00:00:20,160
which simply means the bond that gets cut.

8
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:22,880
Hydrolyzing a peptide bond is favorable,

9
00:00:22,920 --> 00:00:25,600
but on its own the reaction
is extremely slow.

10
00:00:25,640 --> 00:00:28,480
Here is how the enzyme speeds it up.

11
00:00:29,707 --> 00:00:31,107
Into the active site.

12
00:00:31,147 --> 00:00:34,547
Three residues that are far
apart in the sequence meet here:

13
00:00:34,587 --> 00:00:39,767
aspartate 102,
histidine 57 and serine 195,

14
00:00:39,807 --> 00:00:42,347
linked in a network of hydrogen bonds.

15
00:00:42,387 --> 00:00:44,107
This is the catalytic triad.

16
00:00:44,147 --> 00:00:45,947
Serine is the nucleophile.

17
00:00:45,987 --> 00:00:49,707
Histidine is the base that would
take the proton from serine.

18
00:00:49,747 --> 00:00:54,587
Aspartate is hydrogen-bonded to histidine,
and that bond matters in a moment.

19
00:00:54,627 --> 00:00:59,707
Watch the two hydrogen bonds: aspartate
to histidine, histidine to serine.

20
00:01:00,927 --> 00:01:03,127
Now something for the enzyme to cut.

21
00:01:03,167 --> 00:01:06,247
The substrate's phenylalanine
side chain slips into

22
00:01:06,287 --> 00:01:09,927
a deep hydrophobic pocket
beside the active site.

23
00:01:09,967 --> 00:01:12,487
The pocket is lined with nonpolar groups,

24
00:01:12,527 --> 00:01:15,927
so it holds the large aromatic
side chains of phenylalanine,

25
00:01:15,967 --> 00:01:20,497
tyrosine and tryptophan, and that is what
chymotrypsin is selective for.

26
00:01:20,537 --> 00:01:22,657
Fitting the side chain
into the pocket puts

27
00:01:22,697 --> 00:01:26,607
the scissile bond right over serine 195.

28
00:01:27,827 --> 00:01:29,387
Acylation begins.

29
00:01:29,427 --> 00:01:32,347
With the substrate bound,
a subtle change in shape compresses

30
00:01:32,387 --> 00:01:35,947
the hydrogen bond between
aspartate and histidine.

31
00:01:35,987 --> 00:01:38,527
It becomes a low-barrier hydrogen bond,

32
00:01:38,567 --> 00:01:43,577
and the pKa of histidine rises
from about 7 to above 12.

33
00:01:43,617 --> 00:01:48,937
Now histidine is a strong enough base to
take the proton from the serine hydroxyl,

34
00:01:48,977 --> 00:01:52,217
and in the same motion
the serine oxygen attacks

35
00:01:52,257 --> 00:01:55,077
the carbonyl carbon of the scissile bond.

36
00:01:55,117 --> 00:01:58,517
Losing that proton keeps
a positive charge off serine

37
00:01:58,557 --> 00:02:02,307
and turns it into a strongly
nucleophilic alkoxide.

38
00:02:02,557 --> 00:02:06,137
The pi electrons of the carbonyl
move onto the oxygen,

39
00:02:06,177 --> 00:02:09,367
and the carbon goes
from flat to tetrahedral.

40
00:02:09,617 --> 00:02:13,017
Watch the arrows:
in the drawing and in the structure,

41
00:02:13,057 --> 00:02:15,257
they are the same three arrows.

42
00:02:16,427 --> 00:02:20,787
This is the tetrahedral intermediate, and
its oxygen now carries a negative charge.

43
00:02:20,827 --> 00:02:22,707
The enzyme is ready for it.

44
00:02:22,747 --> 00:02:27,677
Two backbone N–H groups point straight
at the oxygen: the oxyanion hole.

45
00:02:27,717 --> 00:02:31,917
It binds the intermediate, and the
transition state that resembles it,

46
00:02:31,957 --> 00:02:34,317
more tightly than it binds the substrate.

47
00:02:34,357 --> 00:02:37,547
That difference is a large
part of the catalysis.

48
00:02:38,767 --> 00:02:40,547
The intermediate collapses.

49
00:02:40,587 --> 00:02:44,647
The electrons on oxygen come back
down to re-form the carbonyl.

50
00:02:44,687 --> 00:02:47,847
Then the scissile bond,
the carbon–nitrogen bond,

51
00:02:47,887 --> 00:02:51,047
breaks, and histidine, now a proton donor,

52
00:02:51,087 --> 00:02:54,547
protonates the amino
group of the leaving half.

53
00:02:54,587 --> 00:02:57,427
The first product, the amine half, leaves.

54
00:02:57,467 --> 00:02:59,987
What stays behind is the acyl-enzyme:

55
00:03:00,027 --> 00:03:04,507
the substrate's carbonyl,
now bonded to serine as an ester.

56
00:03:05,727 --> 00:03:08,647
The second half is the same
chemistry with water.

57
00:03:08,687 --> 00:03:11,927
A water molecule moves
in where the amine was.

58
00:03:11,967 --> 00:03:14,487
Histidine takes a proton from the water,

59
00:03:14,527 --> 00:03:17,617
and the hydroxide attacks
the ester carbonyl.

60
00:03:17,657 --> 00:03:23,457
A second tetrahedral intermediate forms,
held in the same oxyanion hole.

61
00:03:23,497 --> 00:03:28,837
It collapses, serine is displaced
and takes its proton back from histidine,

62
00:03:28,877 --> 00:03:32,037
and the second product,
a carboxylate, is released.

63
00:03:32,077 --> 00:03:35,067
The enzyme is exactly as it started.

64
00:03:36,227 --> 00:03:39,227
This two-step mechanism
shows up in the kinetics.

65
00:03:39,267 --> 00:03:42,427
With an ester substrate
that releases a colored product,

66
00:03:42,467 --> 00:03:44,907
the first molecules come out in a burst,

67
00:03:44,947 --> 00:03:47,977
as fast as the enzyme can be acylated.

68
00:03:48,067 --> 00:03:52,697
Then the rate drops to a steady state,
set by the slower deacylation.

69
00:03:52,867 --> 00:03:56,027
The burst is the acyl-enzyme forming,

70
00:03:56,067 --> 00:03:58,547
and its size matches the amount of enzyme:

71
00:03:58,587 --> 00:04:03,557
each enzyme molecule is acylated
once before the slow step takes over.

72
00:04:04,807 --> 00:04:08,447
Three residues, one pocket,
two backbone N–H groups.

73
00:04:08,487 --> 00:04:11,407
Every arrow you draw for this
mechanism is a motion

74
00:04:11,447 --> 00:04:14,297
of atoms you can find in the structure.

75
00:04:15,527 --> 00:04:18,127
One last question:
how does the pancreas make

76
00:04:18,167 --> 00:04:20,987
an enzyme like this
without digesting itself?

77
00:04:21,027 --> 00:04:24,217
It makes an inactive precursor,
chymotrypsinogen.

78
00:04:24,707 --> 00:04:28,317
In the small intestine,
trypsin cuts just one bond:

79
00:04:28,357 --> 00:04:33,997
here the scissile bond is the one
between arginine 15 and isoleucine 16.

80
00:04:34,037 --> 00:04:36,997
That one cut changes
the shape of the protein:

81
00:04:37,037 --> 00:04:42,117
the hydrophobic pocket forms
and the oxyanion hole is stabilized.

82
00:04:42,157 --> 00:04:48,787
The enzyme is now active, and this first
active form is called pi-chymotrypsin.

83
00:04:49,237 --> 00:04:51,837
Then chymotrypsin trims itself,

84
00:04:51,877 --> 00:04:56,837
cutting out two short pieces:
serine 14 with arginine 15,

85
00:04:56,877 --> 00:05:01,397
and threonine 147 with asparagine 148.

86
00:05:01,437 --> 00:05:03,987
What is left is alpha-chymotrypsin,

87
00:05:04,107 --> 00:05:07,427
the mature enzyme
and the form in our model,

88
00:05:07,467 --> 00:05:10,547
which is why its chain has two small gaps.

